<Project>
  <!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      THE bundle identifier. Everything derives from this one value:

        - the iOS app                    $(ApplicationId)
        - the widget extension           $(ApplicationId).BusWidget
        - the APNs topic the worker uses $(ApplicationId).push-type.liveactivity

      It is here, alone, because a mismatch between any two of those does not
      fail loudly. A wrong APNs topic is a push Apple ACCEPTS and silently drops;
      an extension id not nested under the app's is an install rejection whose
      message does not mention bundle ids. Both cost an evening to find.

      Change it to something your Apple Developer team owns, then re-run
      scripts/build-widget.sh and update Apns__BundleId on the worker (or use
      scripts/print-config.sh, which reads this file).
    -->
    <ApplicationId Condition="'$(ApplicationId)' == ''">com.denlair.whenwillthebus</ApplicationId>

    <!--
      THE Apple Developer Team ID, used to sign everything.

      Here rather than in each script for the same reason as the bundle id: it
      appeared in four of them, and four copies of a value is three chances to
      change only some of them.

      NOT A SECRET. It is in every provisioning profile, in the signature of
      every build, and readable from any installed app. What it identifies is
      the team, not an account, and it authorises nothing on its own.

      The scripts take DEVELOPMENT_TEAM from the environment first and fall
      back to this, announcing which they used, so that a clone of this repository
      on somebody else's Mac is told whose team it is about to try to sign
      with, rather than failing later with a certificate error that does not
      mention it. Find your own with scripts/team-id.sh.
    -->
    <DevelopmentTeam Condition="'$(DevelopmentTeam)' == ''">7NFHX4U9ME</DevelopmentTeam>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
